# Required — src/proxy.ts refreshes the auth session on every navigation and
# @supabase/ssr throws without these, even though no shipped tool needs an account.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=<SUBSTITUTE_SUPABASE_URL>
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=<SUBSTITUTE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY>

# Optional — canonical URLs, Open Graph, and JSON-LD. Falls back to http://localhost:3000.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# Short links — the URL Shortener and the QR tool's dynamic codes, which share
# one short_links table and are the only features that read the database. Blank,
# and the shortener says it has nowhere to store a link while the QR tool's
# "make this code editable" checkbox renders disabled; static QR codes and every
# other tool are unaffected. DIRECT_URL is the non-pooled connection and is only
# needed for db:migrate, db:push, and Prisma Studio — the app never reads it.
DATABASE_URL=
DIRECT_URL=

# Port Scanner — the salt mixed into a visitor's address before it is hashed
# into the port_scan_quota table, so the row says how often somebody scanned and
# never who. Any long random string; `openssl rand -hex 32` will do. Rotating it
# resets every open window, which is the correct failure rather than a bug.
#
# This one does not degrade gracefully and must not: without it — or without
# DATABASE_URL — the scanner refuses every scan rather than running unmetered,
# because the scan leaves this server's address and an unlimited port scanner
# with somebody else's reputation attached is exactly what nobody should host.
PORT_SCAN_IP_SALT=

# Mock Server Studio — the salt mixed into a caller's address before it is
# hashed into the mock_quota table, which meters three things: how many
# workspaces one network may create per hour, how many outbound HTTP requests
# one workspace's graphs may make per hour, and how many requests the public
# endpoints at /m/<key>/ will answer per minute — 120 per calling address and
# 1200 per server. Any long random string; `openssl rand -hex 32` will do.
# Rotating it resets every open window, which is the correct failure.
#
# Like PORT_SCAN_IP_SALT this one does not degrade gracefully and must not:
# without it — or without DATABASE_URL — the studio refuses to create or import
# a workspace, refuses every outbound request an HTTP Request node makes, and
# **refuses to answer on /m/<key>/ at all**, rather than running any of the
# three unmetered. A free, scriptable source of publicly callable endpoints
# under this site's name is exactly what nobody should host, and an unmetered
# execution path is a free way to spend this deployment's whole function budget
# — a front-end with a runaway useEffect does it by accident. The "three
# workspaces per browser" cap is a UI affordance and stops nothing on its own:
# the cookie belongs to the caller.
#
# The studio also needs DATABASE_URL above (no database, and /mock says it has
# nowhere to keep a workspace) and the Turnstile pair below (no site key, and
# creating and importing render disabled).
MOCK_IP_SALT=

# JSON Server Studio — the salt mixed into a caller's address before it is
# hashed into the service_quota table, which meters two things: how many JSON
# servers one network may create per hour, and how many requests the public
# endpoints at /j/<key>/ will answer per minute — 120 per calling address and
# 1200 per server. Any long random string; `openssl rand -hex 32` will do.
# Rotating it resets every open window, which is the correct failure.
#
# Its own variable rather than sharing MOCK_IP_SALT, even though both studios
# count into one table: the digests are namespaced (`json:serve:…` versus
# `mock:serve:…`) so they cannot collide, and one salt per feature is the
# pattern PORT_SCAN_IP_SALT already set.
#
# Like the two salts above this one does not degrade gracefully and must not:
# without it — or without DATABASE_URL — the studio refuses to create or restore
# a server and **refuses to answer on /j/<key>/ at all**, rather than running
# either unmetered. A JSON server stores whatever is posted to it, so an
# unmetered one is free hosting for a stranger's data under this site's name.
# The "five servers per browser" cap is a UI affordance and stops nothing on its
# own: the cookie belongs to the caller.
#
# The studio also needs DATABASE_URL above (no database, and /json says it has
# nowhere to keep a server) and the Turnstile pair below (no site key, and
# creating and restoring render disabled).
JSON_SERVER_IP_SALT=

# GraphQL Server Studio — the salt mixed into a caller's address before it is
# hashed into the service_quota table, which meters two things: how many GraphQL
# servers one network may create per hour, and how many requests the public
# endpoint at /g/<key> will answer per minute — 60 per calling address and 600
# per server. Any long random string; `openssl rand -hex 32` will do. Rotating
# it resets every open window, which is the correct failure.
#
# The per-minute numbers are half the JSON studio's on purpose. A rate limit
# counts requests, and a GraphQL request is not the same size as a REST one:
# `GET /posts` returns one collection, while a single GraphQL document can ask
# for every collection, joined, paged and aliased, up to the node budget the
# query guard allows. Metering those at the same rate would treat a request that
# can be two orders of magnitude more work as though it were the same thing.
#
# Its own variable rather than sharing JSON_SERVER_IP_SALT, even though all
# three studios count into one table: the digests are namespaced
# (`graphql:serve:…` versus `json:serve:…` versus `mock:serve:…`) so they cannot
# collide, and one salt per feature is the pattern PORT_SCAN_IP_SALT set.
#
# Like the salts above it does not degrade gracefully and must not: without it —
# or without DATABASE_URL — the studio refuses to create or restore a server and
# **refuses to answer on /g/<key> at all**, rather than running either
# unmetered. A GraphQL server stores whatever its mutations are given, so an
# unmetered one is free hosting for a stranger's data under this site's name.
# The "five servers per browser" cap is a UI affordance and stops nothing on its
# own: the cookie belongs to the caller.
#
# The studio also needs DATABASE_URL above (no database, and /graphql says it
# has nowhere to keep a server) and the Turnstile pair below (no site key, and
# creating and restoring render disabled).
GRAPHQL_SERVER_IP_SALT=

# Image tools — the salt mixed into a caller's address before it is hashed into
# the service_quota table, which meters the two networked things the seven image
# tools do.
#
# "Paste a picture's address", under the `image:import` namespace: two counters
# per fetch — 20 per minute per calling address, and 60 per minute per target
# host. The second is not about abuse of this site so much as abuse *through*
# it: without it, one link posted somewhere busy turns this deployment into an
# unpaid mirror of somebody else's server.
#
# The Background Remover's stock-background search, under `image:stock`: 40 per
# minute per calling address and 180 per minute across the whole deployment. The
# second counter is the important one here and is deliberately not per host —
# Pexels meters the API key rather than the caller, so every visitor's search
# spends one allowance belonging to this deployment, and without a ceiling one
# busy afternoon takes the picker away from everybody else.
#
# Any long random string; `openssl rand -hex 32` will do. Rotating it resets
# every open window, which is the correct failure. The two namespaces are
# separate digests, so they can never land on one another's rows.
#
# Like the salts above this one does not degrade gracefully and must not:
# without it — or without DATABASE_URL — the URL field is **hidden** on the
# Image Resizer, Compressor, Converter, Blur Placeholder, Background Remover,
# AI Image Detector and Watermark Remover, and the Background Remover's stock
# search renders disabled, rather than either running unmetered. A free,
# anonymous, scriptable HTTP client running from this server's address is a
# proxy long before it is a convenience, and a proxy is what an abuser wants far
# more than a picture is.
#
# Everything else those tools do is unaffected: choosing a file, dropping one
# and pasting one all stay in the reader's tab and never reach this variable —
# as do the Background Remover's cut-out, blur and colour backgrounds.
IMAGE_IMPORT_IP_SALT=

# Background Remover, Photo tab — the API key for Pexels
# (https://www.pexels.com/api/), which backs the stock-background search. Read
# on the server only: the browser calls a Server Action and never Pexels
# directly, because a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name would put a shared, rate-limited
# credential into every page's JavaScript.
#
# Blank, and only that one row degrades: the search field renders disabled and
# says the deployment has no key, while cutting a background out, blurring it,
# painting a colour behind it and dropping in a background of your own all carry
# on working and never touch the network. It also needs IMAGE_IMPORT_IP_SALT and
# DATABASE_URL above for its counter, and refuses rather than searching
# unmetered without them.
#
# Note what the case study in docs/case-studies/background-remover.md says about
# Pexels' own limit: it is charged to this deployment's key, not to a visitor's
# address, so the copy must never describe it as being per visitor.
PEXELS_API_KEY=

# Optional — GA4 measurement id, `G-XXXXXXXXXX`. Even when set, gtag.js only
# loads after the visitor accepts the consent banner AND the build is
# production, so a local clone never reports into the live property.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MEASUREMENT_ID=

# AI Text Detector — Cloudflare Worker fronting @cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct-fast.
# Blank, and only that tool degrades: it renders with its controls disabled and
# says it is not configured. TEXT_DETECTOR_API overrides the NEXT_PUBLIC_ name
# server-side; the browser never calls the worker directly.
TEXT_DETECTOR_API_KEY=<SUBSTITUTE_TEXT_DETECTOR_API_KEY>
NEXT_PUBLIC_TEXT_DETECTOR_API=<SUBSTITUTE_TEXT_DETECTOR_API>

# Cloudflare Turnstile, shared by every tool that calls a model, by both
# short-link creators (the URL Shortener and dynamic QR codes), and by the DNS &
# Domain Inspector. Without the site key each one renders disabled rather than
# unprotected; without the secret every request is refused, because the challenge
# is what keeps the model budget from being drained by a script, what keeps a
# public short-link creator from becoming a phishing service, and what keeps a
# public domain lookup from becoming a free scanner running from this server's
# address. The Domain Inspector reads no other variable: DNS over HTTPS, RDAP,
# Team Cymru's ASN zones and the page probe need nothing but an outbound
# connection.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SECRET=

# AI Image Detector — Cloudflare Worker fronting @cf/meta/llama-4-scout-17b-16e-instruct.
# The endpoint is the worker's /detect URL; a bare origin gets /detect appended.
# Blank, and only that tool degrades, exactly like the text detector.
# FAKE_IMAGE_DETECTOR_API overrides the NEXT_PUBLIC_ name server-side.
FAKE_IMAGE_DETECTOR_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_FAKE_IMAGE_DETECTOR_API=

# Watermark Remover — Cloudflare Worker fronting
# @cf/runwayml/stable-diffusion-v1-5-inpainting. The worker answers POST on
# whatever route it is mounted at, so no path is appended: give the full URL.
# Blank, and only that tool degrades, exactly like the detectors.
# WATERMARK_REMOVER_API overrides the NEXT_PUBLIC_ name server-side; the browser
# never calls the worker directly, which is also why the worker's five-uploads-a
# -minute limit is counted against this server's address rather than a visitor's.
WATERMARK_REMOVER_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_WATERMARK_REMOVER_API=

# Equation to LaTeX Converter, image mode — Cloudflare Worker fronting
# @cf/meta/llama-4-scout-17b-16e-instruct, which transcribes the equations in a
# picture as KaTeX-compatible LaTeX. The worker answers POST on whatever route
# it is mounted at, so no path is appended: give the full URL. MATH_KATEX_API
# overrides the NEXT_PUBLIC_ name server-side; the browser never calls the
# worker directly.
#
# Blank, and only the *image* half degrades: the Image tab renders with its
# controls disabled and says no recognition model is configured, while typing an
# equation carries on working exactly as before — that half is pure browser
# code and reads no variable at all. This is the only tool here where a missing
# key costs a feature rather than the page.
#
# It also needs the Turnstile pair above. Without a site key the Image tab is
# disabled, because a model call costs money and an ungated one is a script's
# free budget. Note what the case study in docs/case-studies/watermark-remover.md
# says about the worker's own per-IP rate limit: the worker sees *this server's*
# address, so its limit is shared by every visitor rather than per person, and
# the copy must never describe it as per visitor.
MATH_KATEX_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_MATH_KATEX_API=

# MCP server — the Model Context Protocol endpoint at /api/mcp, which lets
# Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor and anything else that speaks MCP call the toolbox.
#
# MCP_IP_SALT is not optional in practice. It is mixed into a caller's address
# before it is hashed into `service_quota` under the `mcp:call` namespace, and
# without it (or without DATABASE_URL) the endpoint refuses every request rather
# than running unmetered — an open JSON-RPC endpoint that will run Argon2 and
# generate 4096-bit RSA keys on demand is a scriptable way to spend the whole
# deployment's CPU budget. Any long random string; changing it resets every
# window, which costs a minute.
#
# MCP_ACCESS_TOKEN gates only the MCP tools that make outbound requests —
# currently the Domain Inspector alone. Blank, and those tools refuse with
# `token_missing` while every offline tool (encoding, hashing, JWTs, formatting,
# diffing, …) keeps working for anyone. It is read per request rather than at
# startup, so rotating it takes effect immediately and old clients start failing
# at once, which is the point. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '='
MCP_IP_SALT=
MCP_ACCESS_TOKEN=

PEXELS_API_KEY=